./docs/reviews/2026-07-22-auto-fit-codex.out:1:PRIOR_ART: Verified mainstream practice is “preserve the current/user font, measure the rendered cell, fit integer rows/columns to the container, then resize the PTY/NAWS”—not automatic font shrinking.
./docs/reviews/2026-07-22-auto-fit-codex.out:67:GUARDS: For the default mode, stop at `userFontPref` because it does not shrink. For optional auto/classic fitting, make the readable floor a user/accessibility/profile input. There is no universal evidence-backed CJK minimum; a provisional product default around 12–13 CSS px may be tested, but it must remain configurable and respect browser/OS text scaling. Stop shrinking at that floor, when the requested profile fits, or when a smaller candidate produces the same integer grid. Validate CJK at actual device DPR and font stack. Do not impose an arbitrary application `maxCols`; use the protocol limit (`65535`) plus a separately justified renderer/performance limit. Never report more cells than physically fit, never CSS-scale the canvas, and send NAWS only when the stable integer grid changes.
./docs/reviews/2026-07-22-auto-fit-codex.out:73:4. In `apps/web/src/TerminalHost.tsx::applyFit`, delete the `narrow`, `short`, 16-step shrink loop, and `52×22` objective. Apply user typography once, measure once, floor the real grid, resize the buffer/canvas, and report exactly that grid.
./docs/reviews/2026-07-22-auto-fit-codex.out:74:5. Keep the previous stable fit during transient `0×0` orientation/layout states. Coalesce ResizeObserver/font/visual-viewport events, and emit NAWS only after the resulting `cols×rows` changes.
./docs/reviews/2026-07-21-queue-impl-codex-r3.out:850:        if (ev.option === OPT.NAWS) sendTcp(naws(cols, rows));
